            <ArticleTitle>Lesser Trochanter Migration following Intramedullary Fixation of an Intertrochanteric Femur Fracture.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>9348032</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1155/2016/9348032</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Intertrochanteric femur fractures are commonly observed in the elderly and may be associated with a complete fracture of the lesser trochanter in over 50% of cases. The migration of the lesser trochanter secondary to the psoas muscle contracture is a rare event. This case report presents a rare case of sudden groin pain three-week status after intramedullary fixation of a intertrochanteric femur fracture.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
